.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt HVC 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm hvc ,
.Nm vport
.Nd virtio console
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Sy console Ns = Ns Cm hvc0
.Pp
.Sy console Ns = Ns Cm vport0p Ns Ar N
.Pp
.Pa /dev/hvc0
.Pp
.Pa /dev/vport0p1
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The kernel drives a virtio console device, QEMU's
.Ql virtio-serial-pci ,
as Linux's
.Ql virtio_console
does, and carries its own console over one of the device's ports when
the boot line names it.
On such a machine the console needs no serial port: the log, the debug
shell,
.Xr init 8
and every program on
.Pa /dev/console
read and write the port, a page at a time, where a 16550 moves one byte
at a time.
.Pp
The device has ports.
Port 0 of a device without the multiport feature, and each port the
device marks as a console, QEMU's
.Ql virtconsole ,
is a console port; the first is
.Ql hvc0 .
Any other port, QEMU's
.Ql virtserialport ,
is a generic port, named
.Ql vport0p Ns Ar N
for port
.Ar N .
The driver takes ports 0 and 1 of the first virtio console on the PCI
bus.
.Pp
Each
.Sy console=
on the boot line is read in turn, as Linux reads them.
.Cm hvc0
asks for the first console port and
.Cm vport0p Ns Ar N
for port
.Ar N ,
whatever its kind, which Linux does not offer;
.Cm ttyS0
asks for COM1.
The log goes to every line named, and the console's input comes from
the one named last.
Options after a comma, such as a speed, are not read, and a word that
names neither, such as
.Cm tty0 ,
changes nothing.
Without a
.Sy console=
that names a port the console stays on COM1, as
.Xr console 4
describes it, and the device is bound with no use made of its ports.
.Pp
The port becomes the console when the device announces it during the
boot, for which the boot waits at most two seconds.
On a machine with no COM1 the port is then given every record the log
still holds, from the first of the boot; on a machine with one, the
records so far went to COM1 and the port carries the log from there on.
While the port has the console's input, the port's node is in
.Pa /dev :
.Pa /dev/hvc0
for a console port, character device 229:0, or
.Pa /dev/vport0p1 ,
character device 245:1, for generic port 1.
An open of it gives the console terminal, as an open of
.Pa /dev/console
does, so a login may be started on either name.
.Pp
What is written leaves when a page of it has gathered, at the end of
each record of the log and at the end of each write to the console.
The kernel then waits until the device has taken it, so that a power
off or a reset just after loses nothing.
A host that does not read its end holds the writer for at most 30
seconds; what the four pages in flight cannot hold is then dropped,
and each later write waits 2 milliseconds, until the host reads again.
What the host writes arrives in pages too.
When programs read more slowly than the host writes, the console's
4096 bytes and the port's eight pages fill, and the device stops
reading the host's end until there is room; no byte is dropped.
.Pp
A console port's size, where the device gives one, becomes the console
terminal's window size, and a change of it sends
.Dv SIGWINCH
as
.Dv TIOCSWINSZ
does.
.Pp
During a panic that finds another processor stopped inside the port's
transmit path, each byte is written to the device's emergency write
register instead, where the device has one.
.Pp
The device is shown as
.Pa /sys/bus/virtio/devices/virtio Ns Ar N ,
bound to
.Ql virtio_console .
When it is unbound through
.Pa /sys/bus/pci/drivers/virtio-pci/unbind ,
or its port is removed, the console goes back to COM1; a port added
again, or the device bound again, takes the console back.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/vport0p1"
.It Pa /dev/hvc0
the console, while a console port carries its input
.It Pa /dev/vport0p1
the console, while generic port 1 carries its input
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Start QEMU with the console on a generic port, which loses no output
when the reader of the socket falls behind:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
qemu-system-x86_64 -nodefaults -kernel kernel -initrd test.krffs \e
    -chardev socket,id=con,path=con.sock,server=on \e
    -device virtio-serial-pci -device virtserialport,chardev=con \e
    -append console=vport0p1
.Ed
.Pp
Keep the log on COM1 as well, with the port for input:
.Dl console=ttyS0 console=hvc0
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql virtio_console: bound: virtio0: ports 0 and 1 with the control queues, emergency write
The driver has the device, with what the device offered.
.It Ql console: enabled: vport0p1, port 1 of virtio0
The port is the console from here on;
.Ql , for output alone
follows when COM1 was named after it.
.It Ql console: disabled: vport0p1: its port has gone
The console is back on COM1.
.It Ql console: waiting: vport0p1: virtio0 has not announced it in 2000 ms
The boot went on without the port, which takes the console when the
device announces it.
.It Ql console: none: console=vport0p1 names no port now; the console stays on COM1
The machine has no virtio console, or the device has no such port.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bootlog 4 ,
.Xr console 4 ,
.Xr netconsole 4 ,
.Xr sysfs 5
.Sh STANDARDS
The device is that of the virtio specification, version 1.2, section
5.3, and the driver follows Linux 6.12's
.Pa drivers/char/virtio_console.c
with its hvc console.
Linux gives
.Pa /dev/hvc0
a terminal of its own beside
.Pa /dev/console
and each generic port a byte device with no line discipline; here the
node is the console terminal, and it exists only while the port carries
the console's input.
.Sh BUGS
One device and its ports 0 and 1 are driven; a second console port
would be
.Ql hvc1
and cannot be named on the boot line.
A port that is not the console carries nothing.
There is no
.Pa /proc/consoles ,
and the port has no entry under
.Pa /sys/class/tty .
QEMU's
.Ql virtconsole
discards output when its reader falls behind, and its
.Ql virtserialport
does not; use the second for a console whose every byte counts.
